

                     MSINC, VERSION A4

                       USER'S MANUAL



     ABSTRACT - MSINC, A MODULAR SIMULATOR OF NON-LINEAR ELECTRONIC CIRCUITS,

CAN PERFORM NONLINEAR DC AND TIME-RESPONSE SIMULATION OF MOS TRANSISTOR

CIRCUITS.


                       INTRODUCTION


     MSINC IS PARTICULARLY SUITED FOR ANALYSIS OF NON-LINEAR INTEGRATED

CIRCUITS.  PRESENTLY, IT CAN PERFORM NONLINEAR DC AND TIME-RESPONSE SIMULATION

OF ELECTRONIC CIRCUITS CONTAINING UP TO 100 NODES (EXPLICITLY DEFINED BY

THE USER) AND 100 MOS TRANSISTORS.  ADDITIONAL CIRCUIT ELEMENTS THAT CAN BE

SPECIFIED ARE LINEAR RESISTORS, CAPACITORS, INDUCTORS, DIODES, IDEAL CURRENT

SOURCES AND GROUNDED VOLTAGE SOURCES.  THE SOURCES MAY BE DC, SINUSOIDAL OR

PIECEWISE LINEAR WITH RESPECT TO TIME.  THE TRANSISTOR MODEL IN MSINC INCLUDES

SECOND ORDER DC EFFECTS AND VOLTAGE DEPENDENT CAPACITANCES.  THE ELECTRICAL

PARAMETERS CHARACTERIZING THE CIRCUIT MODELS OF THE MOS TRANSISTORS CAN BE

SPECIFIED BY THE USER.  IF THE USER DOES NOT SPECIFY CERTAIN PARAMETER VALUES,

THE PROGRAM ASSUMES DEFAULT VALUES, WHICH IN MANY INSTANCES SIMPLIFIES THE

MODEL AND REDUCES THE COMPUTATIONAL EFFORT.

     GIVEN A CIRCUIT TOPOLOGY DESCRIPTION AND A FEW ADDITIONAL INSTRUCTIONS,

MSINC CALCULATES AND OUTPUTS THE USER SPECIFIED ELEMENT CURRENTS AND NODE

VOLTAGES AT SIMULATED TIME INTERVALS  SPECIFIED BY THE USER.  THESE OUTPUTS

MAY BE PRINTED EITHER IN TABULAR FORM OR AS MULTIPLE-OUTPUT LINE PRINTER PLOTS

(OR BOTH).  THE DC AND TIME-RESPONSE SIMULATIONS ARE CALCULATED USING THE

NODAL ANALYSIS METHOD.  SPARSE-MATRIX TECHNIQUES ARE USED TO IMPROVE THE

EFFICIENCY OF THE PROGRAM.  FOR USER'S CONVENIENCE, THE INITIAL (DC BIAS)

CONDITIONS FOR THE CIRCUIT ARE AUTOMATICALLY OUTPUTED PRIOR TO THE TIME

RESPONSE SIMULATION.
                    DATA INPUT


     MSINC HAS A SEMI-FREE INPUT FORMAT WHICH MAKES IT EASY TO LEARN TO USE

AND DECREASES THE NUMBER  OF ERROR MESSAGES DUE TO INPUT  MISTAKES.  NO PREVIOUS

COMPUTER PROGRAMMING KNOWLEDGE IS REQUIRED.  DATA LINES MAY BE PUT IN ANY

ORDER, AND THE PARAMETERS ON EACH LINE ARE ENTERED WITH BLANK SPACES TO

DELIMIT VALUES.  CIRCUIT NODE NUMBERS MAY BE ASSIGNED ANY INTEGER VALUE LESS

THAN 100 AND ELEMENTS CAN BE GIVEN ARBITRARY NAMES.  THE GROUND NODE MUST

ALWAYS BE NODE 0 (ZERO).  DATA VALUES CAN BE ENTERED USING "E-FORMAT" OR

ENGINEERING NOTATIONS:

              G FOR 1E9                    M FOR 1E-3

             ME FOR 1E6                   MU FOR 1E-4

              K FOR 1E3                   MI FOR 25.4E-4

                                           U FOR 1E-6

                                           N FOR 1E-9

                                           P FOR 1E-12

     THUS, A CAPACITOR OF 20PF CAN BE SPECIFIED AS  2E-11 OR 20P.  DATA ARE

AUTOMATICALLY CHECKED FOR SYNTAX AND OTHER OBVIOUS ERRORS.  ALL ERRONEOUS

DATA LINES ARE PRINTED OUT WITH THE LOCATION AND NATURE OF THE ERROR(S)

CLEARLY INDICATED TO THE USER.

     TO  ILLUSTRATE HOW THE  DATA ARE INPUTED, A BOOTSTRAP CIRCUIT AND

ITS CORRESPONDING MSINC DATA DESCRIPTION ARE SHOWN IN FIG. 1A AND B.  A

DETAILED DISCUSSION OF THE CARD FORMATS WILL BE PRESENTED IN THE NEXT SECTION.

     IN ADDITIONAL TO THE BASIC FEATURES DESCRIBED ABOVE, MSINC OFFERS

CERTAIN TEMPERATURE ANALYSIS FEATURES WHICH ARE USEFUL FOR IC SIMULATION.

     A CIRCUIT CAN BE ANALYSED FOR UP TO FIVE TEMPERATURES (USE THE TEMP CARD,

SEE BELOW). TEMPERATURE COEFFICIENTS CAN BE SPECIFIED FOR RESISTIVE ELEMENTS

AND FOR SEVERAL TRANSISTOR MODEL PARAMETERS.  MSINC CAN ALSO PERFORM FOURIER

ANALYSIS OF THE OUTPUT WAVEFORMS.
                      INPUT DATA CARD FORMAT



     EACH INPUT CARD MUST BEGIN WITH A KEY CHARACTER OR KEYWORD. THE

INPUT CARD CHARACTERS CAN OCCUR ANYWHERE IN COLUMNS 1 TO 72. EACH

ELEMENT IS IDENTIFIED BY A NAME OF ONE TO THREE CHARACTERS FOLLOWING

EACH KEYWORD.  EACH NAME OF THE SAME KEYWORD MUST BE DISTINCT. DECIMAL

POINTS ARE NOT NECESSARY FOR INTEGER VALUES AND CERTAIN ENGINEERING

NOTATIONS CAN BE USED FOR EXPONENTS. BLANKS CANNOT APPEAR WITHIN A

NAME OR VALUE. A CONTINUATION CARD CAN BE USED ANYWHERE AND SHOULD

HAVE A PLUS(+) AS THE FIRST CHARACTER.




     A) TITLE CARD

          THE FIRST CARD OF THE INPUT CARD DECK.  COLUMN 1-72 MAY BE USED

     FOR COMMENTS TO DESCRIBE THE CIRCUIT ANALYSIS BEING PERFORMED.



     B) COMMENT CARD

          THE GENERAL FORM IS

* COMMENTS

          THE FIRST CHARCTER MUST BE *. THE REST OF THE COLUMNS CAN CONTAIN ANY

     COMMENT.  THIS CARD CAN APPEAR ANYWHERE AFTER THE TITLE CARD AND BEFORE

     END CARD.


     C) CIRCUIT TOPOLOGY

          THIS CONTAINS LINEAR RESISTORS, INDUCTORS, CAPACITORS, DIODES,

     MOS TRANSISTORS AND SUPPLY SOURCES.


     I) R, L AND C ELEMENT SPECIFICATION

          THE GENERAL FORM IS

RXXX N+ N- VALUE TC1 TC2
LXXX N+ N- VALUE TC1 TC2
CXXX N+ N- VALUE TC1 TC2

     WHERE N+ AND N- ARE THE CONNECTING NODES,

           VALUE IS THE ELEMENT VALUE AT 300 DEG. K,

           TC1 AND TC2 ARE THE FIRST AND SECOND ORDER TEMPERATURE COEFFICIENT

                 OF THE ELEMENT VALUE, IN PART PER DEG. K (OPTIONAL).

     EXAMPLES:

R1 1 2 100K
CS 5 0 2.3P
RBE 2 3   5K    .002    5E-5

     II) SOURCE MAY BE CONSTANT, PIECEWISE LINEAR OR SINUSOIDAL.

        1) PIECEWISE LINEAR SOURCE SPECIFICATION

          THE GENERAL FORM IS

VXXX N+ N- V1 T1 V2 T2 V3 T3 ... VN TN
IXXX N+ N- I1 T1 I2 T2 I3 T3 ... IN TN

          THE FIRST CHARACTER (KEYWORD) SPECIFIES THE SOURCE TO BE A

     VOLTAGE (V) OR A CURRENT (I) SUPPLY,

          FOR VOLTAGE SOURCES N+ IS THE POSITIVE NODE AND N- IS THE NEGATIVE

     NODE. N- MUST BE ZERO (GROUND).

          FOR CURRENT SOURCES THE CURRENT FLOWS FROM THE NODE N+, THROUGH

     THE SOURCE, TO THE NODE N-.

          VI IS THE VOLTAGE AT THE ITH TIMEPOINT, TI  AND

          II IS THE CURRENT AT THE ITH TIMEPOINT, TI.

          A MAXIMUM OF NINE BREAKPOINTS ARE ALLOWED FOR EACH SOURCE. T1

     MUST BE ZERO.


        2) CONSTANT SOURCE SPECIFICATION

          THE GENERAL FORM IS

VXXX N+ N- V1
IXXX N+ N- I1

          THE NOTATION IS THE SAME AS FOR PIECEWISE LINEAR SOURCE.

          V1 (I1) IS THE VOLTAGE (CURRENT) VALUE.

     EXAMPLES:

VSIN 2 0 1 0 -.5 2MS
I3 3 4 2M 0 0 1N 0 10N 2M 11N 2M 50N
VCC 9 0 5V
VIN 1 0    0   0U    0   2U    5   4U    5  10U
+          0  12U    0  20U    5  22U    5  50U

        3) SINUSOIDAL SOURCE SPECIFICATION

          THE GENERAL FORM IS

VSIN N+ N- VM F PHI DC
ISIN N+ N- IM F PHI

     WHERE VSIN(ISIN) SPECIFIES THE SOURCE TO BE SINUSOIDAL VOLTAGE (CURRENT)

                      SOURCE,

           VM OR IM IS THE AMPLITUDE OF THE SOURCE,

           F IS THE FREQUENCY OF THE SOURCE IN HZ,

           PHI IS THE PHASE ANGLE IN DEGREES, AND

           DC IS THE DC LEVEL ( FOR VOLTAGE SOURCE ONLY).


          THE SOURCE VALUE AT TIME T IS CALCULATED ACCORDING TO

     V(T) = VM * SIN (2 * PI * (F * T + PHI/360)) + DC
     I(T) = IM * SIN (2 * PI * (F * T + PHI/360))

     EXAMPLES:

VSIN 2 0 10M 100KHZ 0 1.5V
ISIN 3 5 1M 2MEGHZ 0

     III) DIODE SPECIFICATION

          THE GENERAL FORM IS

DXXX N+ N- ISS CJO TAU PHI

     WHERE N+ IS THE NODE CONNECTED TO THE P-SIDE OF THE DIODE AND

           N- IS THE N-SIDE NODE,

           ISS IS THE REVERSE SATURATION CURRENT CURRENT (DEFAULT=1E-14)

           CJO IS THE DIODE CAPACITANCE AT ZERO VOLTAGE BIAS

              (DEFAULT = 0 )

           TAU IS THE TRANSIT TIME (DEFAULT = 0 )

           PHI IS THE JUNCTION POTENTIAL (DEFAULT = 0.6)

     EXAMPLES :

D2 2 0
DIOD 2 3 .001P 1P 10N .58
D17 17 0 / 2P

     IV) TRANSISTOR SPECIFICATION

          THE GENERAL FORM IS

QXXX MXXX ND NG NS NB Z L RD RS ADB ASB VIGS VIDS VISB

     WHERE Q IS THE KEYWORD FOR NAME OF THE MOS TRANSISTOR

           B (OR M) IS THE KEYWORD FOR NAME OF THE MODEL OF THE TRANSISTOR,

           ND, NG, NS AND NB ARE THE DRAIN NODE, GATE NODE, SOURCE NODE

                 AND BULK (SUBSTRATE) NODE RESPECTIVELY,

           Z AND L ARE THE MASK CHANNEL WIDTH AND LENGTH RESPECTIVELY ( THE

                 NET CHANNEL LENGTH WILL BE THE MASK CHANNEL LENGTH LESS

                 TWICE THE LATERAL DIFFUSION).

           RD AND RS ARE THE DRAIN AND SOURCE RESISTANCES RESPECTIVELY.

                 IF ZERO IS INPUTED, OR VALUE IS UNSPECIFIED, THE CORRESPONDING

                 RESISTOR IS ASSUMED TO BE NON-EXISTENT.

           ADB AND ASB ARE THE DRAIN-BULK JUNCTION AREA AND SOURCE-BULK

                 RESPECTIVELY. IF UNSPECIFIED, ZERO AREA (NO JUNCTION

                 CAPACITANCE) IS ASSUMED.

           VIGS, VIDS AND VISB ARE THE INITIAL ESTIMATES OF GATE-SOURCE

                 VOLTAGE, DRAIN-SOURCE VOLTAGE AND SOURCE-BULK VOLTAGE

                 RESPECTIVELY.  THIS HELPS IN SETTING UP THE DC SOLUTION FOR

                 THE CIRCUIT.  FOR P-CHANNEL DEVICE, THE SIGN OF THE VOLTAGES

                 SHOULD BE INVERTED.  IF UNSPECIFIED, A BIAS OF 2V, 1V AND

                 0V ARE ASSUMED RESPECTIVELY. THIS SPECIFICATION WILL BE

                 OVERWRITTEN BY STARTING VOLTAGE SPECIFICATION (SEE BELOW).


     EXAMPLES:

Q1 M1 7 6 2 0    10M 1M 0 0 2P 2P
QXYZ   MP  4  4  3  9  200E-4  15E-4
QOFF  BDEP 2 3 3 1 20M .8M  0 0   1P 1P   0 2 0
     D) MODEL CARD

          THE SIMPLEST MODEL SPECIFICATION CARD CONSISTS OF THE FOLLOWING

BXXX TYPE

     WHERE TYPE IS EITHER PMO (P-CHANNEL DEVICE) OR NMO (N-CHANNEL DEVICE).
           IF TYPE IS UNSPECIFIED, PMO IS ASSUMED.

          THE MODEL CAN BE SPECIFIED IN FULL AS FOLLOWS:

BXXX TYPE VTO=VALUE UB=VALUE,EQ#,FIELD1,FIELD2,ESAT COX=VALUE
+ DNB=VALUE CJUN=VALUE XD=VALUE XS=VALUE XN=VALUE
+ GDS=EQ#,ARG1,ARG2 CSS=VALUE TEM=VALUE

          THE PARAMETERS CAN APPEAR AS GROUPS IN ANY ORDER.  ANY PARAMETER

                 GROUP CAN BE OMITTED, IN WHICH CASE THE DEFAULT VALUES (SEE

                 BELOW) ARE ASSUMED.  THE VALUE SPECIFICATIONS IN ANY GROUP

                 (TO THE RIGHT OF THE PARAMETER NAME) SHOULD BE PUT IN THE

                 ORDER INDICATED.  OMITTING LEADING VALUES SHOULD BE INDICATED

                 BY A SLASH (/).

NAME      PARAMETER                                     DEFAULT

VTO       THRESHOLD VOLTAGE WITH SOURCE GROUNDED        0
UB        LOW FIELD MOBILITY VALUE                      450
  EQ#     CHOICE OF MOBILITY EQUATION                   0
           0 => NO GATE FIELD DEPENDENT MOBILITY
           1 => UB = UB0 / (1 + FIELD1*(VGS-VFB'-VDS/2))
           2 => FROHMAN'S EQUATION
  FIELD1  FIRST MOBILITY COEFFICIENT                    0
  FIELD2  SECOND MOBILITY COEFFICIENT                   0
  ESAT    CRITICAL FIELD FOR VELOCITY SATURATION        0
           0 => NO VELOCITY SATURATION
COX       OXIDE CAPACITANCE PER SQ.CM.                  3E-8
DNB       DOPING CONCENTRATION IN SUBSTRATE PER CU. CM. 1E15
CJUN      JUNCTION CAPACITANCE PER UNIT AREA, USED      1
           TOGETHER WITH ADB AND ASB IN TRANSISTOR CARD
XD        OVERLAP OF DRAIN LENGTH UNDER GATE IN CM.     0
XS        OVERLAP OF SOURCE LENGTH UNDER GATE IN CM.    0
XN        LATERAL DIFFUSION UNDER GATE IN CM.           0
GDS       CHOICE OF DEPLETION EQUATION                  0
               0 - NO CHANNEL LENGTH DEPLETION
               1 - SQUARE ROOT DEPENDENCE
               2 - FRINGING FIELD
  ARG1    FIRST COEFFICIENT                             0.2
  ARG2    SECOND COEFFICIENT                            0.6
CSS       SATURATION CURRENT OF JUNCTION DIODE          1E-14
TEM       PARAMETER DEFINITION TEMPERATURE              300

     EXAMPLES:

B1 PMO VTO=-4 UB=230,/,.15 COX=3.3E-8
+ XD=0.07M XS=0.07M
BP PMO VTO=-3
     E) CONTROL

          THIS INCLUDES THE TIME CARD, TEMP CARD, ALTER CARD, TOLERANCE

     CARD, SHOW CHARACTERISTICS, STARTING VOLTAGE SPECIFICATION AND

     END CARD


     I) TIME CARD

          THE GENERAL FORM IS

TIME DELT TAT

     WHERE DELT IS THE TIME INTERVAL AT WHICH THE OUTPUTS ARE PRINTED,

           TAT IS THE TOTAL ANALYSIS TIME.

          IF THIS CARD IS ABSENT, ONLY DC ANALYSIS IS PERFORMED.

     EXAMPLE:

TIME .5N 20N




     II) TEMP CARD

          THE GENERAL FORM IS

TEMP T1 T2 ...

     WHERE T1, T2 ARE THE TEMPERATURES AT WHICH THE ANALYSES ARE TO BE

          PERFORMED, IN UNITS OF DEGREE K.

          A TOTAL OF UP TO FIVE DIFFERENT TEMPERATURES CAN BE SPECIFIED.

                  T1 IS TAKEN AS THE NOMINAL TEMPERATURE.

          IF THIS CARD IS ABSENT, THE TEMPERATURE IS ASSUMED TO BE AT

                  300 DEGREE K.

     EXAMPLE:

TEMP 300 295 315

     III) ALTER CARD

          THE FORM IS

ALTR

          THIS MUST PRECEDED THE END CARD.

          AFTER ALTR, ANY ELEMENT OR MODEL CAN BE SPECIFIED.

          IF THE NAME OF AN ELEMENT IS THE SAME AS ONE BEFORE ALTR,  THE NEW

                  DATA WILL REPLACE THE OLD,

          IF THE NAME OF AN ELEMENT IS NEW, THIS IS ADDED TO THE CIRCUIT

                  TOPOLOGY.

          UP TO A TOTAL OF THREE ALTR CARDS CAN BE ALLOWED IN EACH SIMULATION.




     IV) TOLERANCE CARD

          THE GENERAL FORM IS

TOL TOLER

     WHERE TOLER IS THE ERROR ALLOWED.

          IF THE DIFFERENCES IN NODE VOLTAGES BETWEEN TWO ITERATIONS ARE LESS

                  THAN TOLER, SOLUTION IS OBTAINED.

          IF UNSPECIFIED, A VALUE OF 1.E-5 (10 MICRO-VOLTS) IS ASSUMED.

     EXAMPLE:

TOL .1UV

     V) SHOW CHARACTERISTICS CARD

          THE FORM IS

SHOW

          THIS CARD IS USED WHEN THE CHARACTERISTICS OF A TRANSISTOR IS TO

                BE PLOTTED OUT. THIS FEATURE REQUIRES SPECIAL INPUT DECK

                SET-UP. THE TRANSISTOR IS SET SO THAT ITS FOUR TERMINAL ARE

                ALL CONNECTED TO VOLTAGE SOURCES. ONE OF THE VOLTAGE SOURCES

                (CONTROL SOURCE) HAS A RAMP WAVESHAPE WHILE THE OTHER THREE

                ARE CONNECTED TO CONSTANT VOLTAGES. THE CHARACTERISTICS ARE

                SPECIFIED BY THE PLOT CARD. N+ SPECIFIES THE VARIABLE TO BE

                PLOTTED OUT.


N+              VARIABLE

1               DRAIN CURRENT (IDS)

2               TRANSCONDUCTANCE (GM) - CHANGE OF CURRENT WRT GATE-SOURCE
                        VOLTAGE
3               CONDUCTANCE (GG) - CHANGE OF CURRENT WRT DRAIN-SOURCE
                        VOLTAGE
4               BACK-GATE TRANSCONDUCTANCE (GMB) - CHANGE OF CURRENT WRT
                        SOURCE-BULK VOLTAGE
5               INVERSE TRANSCONDUCTANCE (GMI) - CHANGE OF CURRENT WRT
                        GATE-DRAIN VOLTAGE (INVERSE MODE OF OPERATION)
6               INVERSE BACK-GATE TRANSCONDUCTANCE (GMBI) - CHANGE OF CURRENT
                        WRT DRAIN-BULK VOLTAGE (INVERSE MODE)
7               DRAIN-SOURCE SATURATION VOLTAGE (VDSAT)

8               GATE-BULK CAPACITANCE (CGB)
9               GATE-DRAIN CAPACITANCE (CGD)
10              GATE-SOURCE CAPACITANCE (CGS)

          AN INPUT EXAMPLE OF THE DRAIN CURRENT, TRANSCONDUCTANCE,
                CONDUCTANCE AND BACK-GATE TRANSCONDUCTANCE, GATE-TO-BULK,
                GATE-TO-DRAIN AND GATE-TO-SOURCE CAPACITANCES WITH
                RESPECT TO DRAIN-TO-SOURCE VOLTAGE IS SHOWN :

CHARACTERISTICS WRT VDS
Q1 M1 1 2 3 0 10M .5M
M1 PMO VTO=-2
VD 1 0 0 0 -10 10U
VG 2 0 -10
VS 3 0 0
TIME .2U 10U
PLOT IDS 1 0
PLOT GM 2 0 GG 3 0 GMB 4 0
PLOT CGB 8 0 CGD 9 0 CGS 10 0
SHOW
END


     VI) STARTING VOLTAGE SPECIFICATION

          THE GENERAL FORM IS

START N1 V1  N2 V2  ...

     WHERE N1, N2, ... ARE THE NODE NUMBERS AND

           V1, V2, ... ARE THE CORRESPONDING STARTING VOLTAGES.

          THE PROGRAM WILL START WITH VOLTAGES AS SPECIFIED. FOR NODES

                THAT ARE NOT SPECIFIED, ZERO VOLT IS ASSUMED TO BE THE

                STARTING POINT UNLESS IT IS A VOLTAGE SOURCE NODE. FOR

                VOLTAGE SOURCE CONNECTED NODES, THE DC VOLTAGES ARE

                ALWAYS UESD AS THE STARTING VALUES.

          THIS SPECIFICATION WILL OVERRIDE THE INITIAL VOLTAGE

                ESTIMATE IN THE TRANSISTOR SPECIFICATION.

          THIS SPECIFICATION CAN BE DONE WITH SEVERAL STARTING VOLTAGE

                CARDS, WITH CONTINUATION CARDS, OR BOTH.

     EXAMPLE :

START 1 5.0 2 3 5 10.0 6 2.0
+ 15 0  16 0.0


     VII) END CARD

          THE FORM IS

END

          THE END CARD MUST BE THE LAST CARD OF THE INPUT DECK.  COLUMN 1-3

                  MUST CONTAIN THE LETTERS END.  COLUMN 4-72 MAY CONTAIN ANY

                  COMMENT.
     F) OUTPUT

          THIS INCLUDES THE PRINT CARD(S), PLOT CARD(S) AND FOURIER ANALYSIS

     CARD.


     I) PRINTED OUTPUT CARD

          THE GENERAL FORM IS

PRNT XXXX N+ N-
PRNT XXXX YYYY

     WHERE XXXX IS THE NAME OF THE OUTPUT VARIABLE

          FOR VOLTAGE OUTPUT, THE NODES ( N+ AND N-) ARE SPECIFIED

          FOR CURRENT OUTPUT, THE NAME OF THE ELEMENT (YYYY) THROUGH

                WHICH THE CURRENT FLOWS IS SPCIFIED

          NOTE THAT EACH CARD SPECIFIES ONE OUTPUT VARIABLE

          A TOTAL OF UP TO NINE OUTPUT CARDS ARE ALLOWED TO EACH INPUT DECK.

     EXAMPLES:

PRNT VOUT 5 9
PRNT IR4 R4


     II) PLOT CARD

          THE GENERAL FORM IS

PLOT MIN MAX XXX1 ZZZ1 XXX2 ZZZ2 XXX3 ZZZ3 XXX4 ZZZ4

     WHERE XXX1, XXX2, XXX3 AND XXX4 ARE THE NAMES OF THE OUTPUT VARIABLES,

           ZZZ1, ZZZ2, ZZZ3 AND ZZZ4 SPECIFIES VOLTAGE OUTPUTS (IF TWO NODE

           NUMBERS ARE SPECIFIED) OR CURRENT OUTPUT (IF NAME OF AN ELEMENT

           IS SPECIFIED).

           EACH OF THE THE PAIRS, EXCEPT XXX1 AND ZZZ1, ARE OPTIONAL AND ARE

           SPECIFIED ONLY IF THE OUTPUT IS NEEDED. THUS, THERE IS A MAXIMUM

           FOUR GRAPHS PER OUTPUT PLOT.

           A MAXIMUM OF SIX OUTPUT PLOTS ARE ALLOWED PER SIMULATION. THUS,

           A TOTAL OF TWENTY-FOUR GRAPHS (FOUR PER PLOT) CAN BE SPECIFIED.


           MIN AND MAX ARE THE COORDINATE SPECIFICATIONS OF THE PLOT, IN

                  VOLTS FOR VOLTAGES AND MILLIAMPERES FOR CURRENTS.  IF MIN

                  AND MAX ARE UNSPECIFIED, THE LIMITS WILL BE DETERMINED BY

                  THE PROGRAM.

          THE MAXIMUM NUMBER OF POINTS TO BE PLOTTED IS LIMITED TO THE FIRST

                  200 TIME INTERVALS AS SPECIFIED ON THE TIME CARD.


     EXAMPLES:

PLOT -5 5 VOUT 10 0 VC2 3 0
PLOT 0 1 IRS 1 2 IOUT 8 9
PLOT VOUT 8 0 VIN 1 0

     III) FOURIER ANALYSIS CARD

          THE GENERAL FORM IS

FOR TSTAR TSTP NHAR

     WHERE TSTAR AND TSTP ARE THE START AND THE STOP TIMES FOR THE TIME INTERVAL

                  WHICH IS ASSUMED TO BE THE PERIOD,

           NHAR IS THE NUMBER OF HARMONICS DESIRED. NHAR MUST BE LESS THAN OR

                  EQUAL TO 49.

          FOURIER ANALYSIS IS PERFORMED FOR ALL THE PLOT OUTPUTS.

     EXAMPLE:

FOR .2U .5U 5
                  DEBUGGING (OR TROUBLESHOOTING)

     THE PROGRAM, MSINC, IS WRITTEN TO MAKE DEBUGGING EASY.  THE METHOD

SUGGESTED IS AS FOLLOWS:

     1) IF THERE ARE FORMAT ERROR(S) THE ERROR MESSAGE WILL BE PRINTED

          AFTER THE STATEMENT, PRECEDED BY ****.

     2) REDRAW THE CIRCUIT DIAGRAM FROM THE DATA INPUT PRINTED BY THE

          COMPUTER TO CHECK IF THERE ARE WRONG NODE CONNECTIONS.

     3) ON THE SECOND PAGE, THERE IS A LIST OF ALL PARAMETER

          VALUES

          I) CHECK THE MODEL PARAMETER VALUES CAREFULLY. THE VALUES ARE

             IN E FORMAT, SEE IF ANY MAGNITUDE IS IN ERROR.

         II) CHECK THE SOURCE SPECIFICATIONS, BOTH VALUES AND TIMES, SEE

             IF THE INPUT WAVEFORM IS THE SHAPE YOU WANT.

     4) ON THE THIRD PAGE, THERE WILL BE A LIST OF INITIAL NODAL

          VOLTAGES.  SEE IF THERE ARE DISCREPANCIES FROM  YOUR HAND

          CALCULATED OR ESTIMATED VALUES.

     5) CAUTION MUST BE EXERCISED IN ASSUMING THE VALIDITY OF SIMULATED

          WAVEFORMS.  TIME SCALE CHANGES CAN OFTEN REVEAL WAVEFORM FEATURES

          INITIALLY OVERLOOKED.  ALTHOUGH PROGRAM CONVERGENCE IS QUITE GOOD,

          THE USER SHOULD BE CAUTIONED THAT CONVERGENCE DOES NOT STRICTLY

          MEAN ACCURATE RESULTS.

     6) ASK FOR HELP IF YOU CANNOT TRACE THE SOURCE OF THE ERROR. CONSULT

          ANYONE YOU KNOW WHO ARE EXPERIENCED WITH CIRCUIT SIMULATION.
